=== Psalm 26
--- Psalm 26 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 26
sub: Right judgment • In the congregations will I bless the LORD
refs: Psalm 26:1

--- Judge not
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 26:1
scripture: Matthew 7:1-2 | mark: Judge not, that ye be not judged
p: Jesus' words are often twisted by others to mean a Christian should never judge another person, no matter how wicked they are or what sins they do. But, we know the warning is against hypocrisy, not right judgment.

--- Judge me, O Lord
kicker: Psalm 26 · Right judgment
scripture: Psalm 26:1-2 | mark: Judge me, O LORD; Examine me, O LORD, and prove me
p: **Psalm 26** gives us a picture of what Jesus is actually teaching, where David asks the Lord to judge him by his doings, according to his faith, in comparison to the wicked around him.
p: The understanding of proper judgement is key to good Christian living. It allows us to be separated, to be holy as God is holy, to put away the filth of the flesh and walk in the Spirit. To seek to be a disciplined servant of the Lord.

--- I shall not slide
kicker: Psalm 26 · Integrity
p: We walk in integrity, and trust in the Lord alone, that we shall not slide. Firmly on His rock, not backsliding to live as the world does.
p: It is not an automatic transformation. We are sanctified by the Spirit by the inward man, the new man, but our flesh is still prone to lusts of the eyes and the pride of life and we can stumble and fall.
p: But if we seek the judgment of God, asking Him to try us and see that our heart is right, we are opening ourselves to correction and refinement, to better be used by God.

--- I have walked in thy truth
kicker: Psalm 26 · Thy lovingkindness
big: Psalm 26:3
p: Walking in His truth, keeping ourselves pure and humble, seeing others in the way God does, with grace and mercy and lovingkindness, we can make our case to the Lord, and compare ourselves to the wicked without the leaven of the Pharisees, hypocrisy.

--- I have not sat with vain persons
kicker: Psalm 26 · Separation
scripture: Psalm 26:4-5 | mark: I have not sat with vain persons, neither will I go in with dissemblers
p: David lists the things he has not done, to separate himself towards God.
p: He has not sat with vain persons, false believers. He has not had ecumenical fellowship with the dissemblers, the false prophets who teach lies. He has avoided the congregation of evil doers. The bitter fruit of the false religions of this world. He has not yoked with unbelievers.

--- So will I compass thine altar
kicker: Psalm 26 · Innocency
big: Psalm 26:6 | mark: so will I compass thine altar
p: Instead, knowing he is innocent of these things, and sanctified, he is able to come before the altar of the Lord. To gather with the assembly of God's people. To walk into church without the burden of unresolved sin, with a good conscience towards God.
p: He doesn't do this to be haughty, or to puff up himself in comparison to the wicked, but that he may proclaim the goodness of the Lord in the midst of the congregation, without hypocrisy.

--- The habitation of thy house
kicker: Psalm 26 · Thy house
big: Psalm 26:8 | mark: I have loved the habitation of thy house
p: Meekly seeking God's judgment in our lives that we may be a better testimony is a good thing. Judgment that leads to repentance and forgiveness of others, that we may love the habitation of His house, that we may know how to behave in the church, as a pillar and foundation of truth, is a good thing.

--- Gather not my soul with sinners
kicker: Psalm 26 · Redeem me
scripture: Psalm 26:9-11 | mark: Gather not my soul with sinners; redeem me, and be merciful unto me
p: Asking the Lord to remember us, that we aren't caught up in His judgment of the wicked, but rather walk in our integrity, waiting for His redemption out of His wrath, seeking His mercy in our obedience.

--- An even place
kicker: Psalm 26 · In the congregations
big: Psalm 26:12
p: This should be our guiding principle. To rightly judge, beginning with ourselves that we may purge ourselves, be separated unto the Lord that we may boldly proclaim His mercy and bless the Lord in the congregation. Amen.

--- That we go to church!
kicker: Application • The assembly
p: Fundamentally, **Psalm 26** is an encouragement to purify ourselves and avoid hypocrisy while recognizing the evil of men, that we may go into the assembly sanctified.
p: But this also presupposes that we are to go into the assembly. That we are not to forsake the assembly. That we go to church!
p: We have an obligation to bless the Lord among the brethren. To gather and sing praises towards Him. Because He has judged us, found us wanting, and yet while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us. That in God's lovingkindness, He made provisions of grace to redeem and reconcile us.

--- Forsaking the assembly
kicker: Application • The assembly
p: For this we should want to sing His praises. But often, if we don't allow God's judgment willingly, if we don't purify ourselves, if we gather with dissemblers, and allow our own hearts to mislead us, and justify ourselves despite our sin, we may find ourselves forsaking the assembly, not going in to the congregation, skipping church.
p: Our conscience may be pricked, and there will be preaching against sin. It is easier to backslide when you do. And the feedback loop compounds, until you are among the vain persons.

--- His mercies are new each morning
kicker: Application • The assembly
p: If you don't willfully seek the judgment of God in your life, He will still judge you. And in a backslidden state, a Christian can be caught up with the bloody men, and lose their life. Not their eternal life, certainly, but cut short in this one.
p: Seeking to avoid the refiner's fire of God's judgment can result in far greater pain and loss in His chastening than in the embarrassment and humbling of ourselves when we get right and submit ourselves to His ways.
p: Be ready daily to submit to His judgment. His mercies are new each morning. It's never too late to get right while you're on this side of Heaven. Allow God to judge your walk, and stand in your integrity, that you may enter gleefully into the congregation.

--- The voice of thanksgiving
kicker: Psalm 26 · Thy wondrous works
big: Psalm 26:7

=== Psalm 27
--- Psalm 27 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 27
sub: The LORD is my light and my salvation • Seek ye my face
refs: Psalm 27:1

--- Whom shall I fear?
kicker: Psalm 27 · My light and my salvation
big: Psalm 27:1 | mark: The LORD is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear?
p: **Psalm 27** continues the pattern of praise for the salvation found in the Lord, by faith.
p: The Lord is our light and salvation. Jesus is the way, the truth and the light. We who have trusted in Him, are kept by the power of God unto salvation. What do we have to fear from men?
scripture: Luke 12:4-5 | mark: Be not afraid of them that kill the body

--- In this will I be confident
kicker: Psalm 27 · Though war should rise
scripture: Psalm 27:2-3 | mark: though war should rise against me, in this will I be confident
p: The wicked who may seek your life cannot do anything but threaten you with Heaven. Whatever battle we face, we are already on the winning side. All in Christ have overcome this world. We can be confident in this, unto death.
p: And praise God we aren't in a time where we face constant war and battles. That our greatest fight is spiritual. And against our own flesh.

--- The mission field
kicker: Application • Without fear
p: But there are those in the mission field, in countries where you can be beaten, imprisoned and killed for the cause of Christ. Meek and humble men and women who do great things for the Lord, daily. Winning souls and spreading the word of God in the face of persecution, without fear.
p: Many Christians today won't even venture into safe suburban neighborhoods once a week to do the same. Often, out of fear.
p: The fear of being a peculiar person. Of being socially rejected for the audacity of preaching Jesus to strangers. Of the "danger" of someone mocking you, or shutting the door in your face.

--- Faith over fear
kicker: Application • Without fear
p: David believed, and it was sufficient to face legions of enemies. To face a giant with a sling. Missionaries and their converts face arrest and imprisonment, beatings and death.
p: American Christians wear "Faith Over Fear" graphic tees to the gym and think they are making a bold stand for Christ.
p: We should be boldly proclaiming the trust we have in a mighty God and the reward He has promised through the Son. *Though war should rise against me, in this will I be confident.*

--- One thing have I desired
kicker: Psalm 27 · The house of the Lord
scripture: Psalm 27:4-6 | mark: One thing have I desired of the LORD; he shall set me up upon a rock
p: Continuing Psalm 27, after establishing the faith on which he stands, and the confidence he has on the promises of God, David as psalmist cries out to the Lord for His mercy, to hear his prayer.

--- Hear, O Lord, when I cry
kicker: Psalm 27 · Have mercy upon me
big: Psalm 27:7
p: As these are meant to be sung or said, as model prayers or devotional worship, we are called to do the same. To call upon the mercy of the Lord. To bless His longsuffering and grace towards us, and petition the Lord with prayer.
big: Hebrews 11:6 | mark: he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him

--- Seek ye my face
kicker: Psalm 27 · Thy face, Lord, will I seek
big: Psalm 27:8 | mark: Seek ye my face
p: When we seek the Lord, it is obedience to His command to seek His face. To search for Him. As individuals and as a nation of believers.
big: 2 Chronicles 7:14 | mark: and seek my face

--- Seek and ye shall find
kicker: Psalm 27 · Thy face, Lord, will I seek
p: *Seek and ye shall find*, the Bible says. Our God is merciful to hear our prayers and to reward those that in faith, have sought Him out. What a humbling and amazing thing to be considered worthy of attention by our Creator.

--- O God of my salvation
kicker: Psalm 27 · Leave me not
big: Psalm 27:9 | mark: leave me not, neither forsake me, O God of my salvation
p: David continues, meekly asking for forgiveness if there are any transgressions that may have displeased the Lord. Asking for His favor, to not forsake us in times of trouble, yet having the understanding that our trouble may be due to our own unresolved sin. Either way, *O God of* our *salvation,* hear our prayers.

--- The Lord will take me up
kicker: Psalm 27 · When my father and my mother forsake me
big: Psalm 27:10
p: In the very next breath, David affirms the steadfast faithfulness of the Lord, that even if our own parents forsake us, He remains true.
p: Expressing fear and doubt while recognizing the truth of the promises of God is a human thing, and we see this play out on our own minds, even as it does David's. We are a weak and needy people, and we need God involved in every aspect of our lives.

--- A way to escape
kicker: Psalm 27 · Teach me thy way
big: Psalm 27:11 | mark: lead me in a plain path
p: Remember this, and be open to His teaching. Be open to hearing His voice in scripture, recognizing the chastening in our life, seeing His mercy towards us, and wrath revealed from Heaven, and fearing Him, always.
p: He shows us the path. He gives us a way out of every temptation.
big: 1 Corinthians 10:13 | mark: make a way to escape

--- False witnesses
kicker: Psalm 27 · Mine enemies
big: Psalm 27:12
p: We may ask for His help to see the deliverance He has for us, even as our adversaries seek to destroy us with their lies and schemes.

--- I had fainted, unless I had believed
kicker: Psalm 27 · Wait on the Lord
scripture: Psalm 27:13-14 | mark: I had fainted, unless I had believed; Wait on the LORD: be of good courage
p: The evidence of God's grace and mercy to His people gives us faith and strength to carry on. This is a key reason why it is important to praise Him in the congregation, to edify those going through the trials of life, to have hope. That all things work together for good. To wait, patiently for the Lord to move. To be of good courage, knowing what He has done for others and has done for us in the past. Knowing our present suffering leads to the glory we are partakers in, through Christ.

--- More than conquerors
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 27:13–14
scripture: Romans 8:35-37 | mark: we are more than conquerors through him that loved us
center: Amen
big: 1 Chronicles 16:11

--- Because they choose to
kicker: Application • Soul winning
p: Last night after Wednesday service, a few of the brothers, myself and our Pastor were talking, and the subject of soul-winning came up. It was part of a broader theme of those who choose to reject 3-to-thrive, as Pastor said, because they wanted to. Who don't tithe or give, because they choose not to. They don't read their Bible, because they choose not to. People who never go door knocking and never share the gospel, because they want to do something else...And how they backslide and struggle with sin, because they choose to.
p: We are going to once again make these things a particular point of emphasis, especially among the men. It can be a struggle to get some Christians to choose God over family, let alone football and recreation and sitting on the couch watching TV.

=== Psalm 28
--- Psalm 28 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 28
sub: The LORD is my strength and my shield
refs: Psalm 28:1

--- Unto thee will I cry
kicker: Psalm 28 · My rock
scripture: Psalm 28:1-2 | mark: Unto thee will I cry, O LORD my rock
p: The Lord is our rock and our salvation. To Him we cry in trouble.
p: Here in **Psalm 28**, David again shows the importunity of prayer, reminding God of what He means to us, and how His promises to deliver His children, and make us holy, also carry promises of the destruction of the wicked.
p: Reminding God of our understanding, reinforces the truth in our hearts and provides ready comfort. Because the world is filled with the wicked, and it is easy to become despondent over it.

--- Mischief is in their hearts
kicker: Psalm 28 · The workers of iniquity
big: Psalm 28:3 | mark: which speak peace to their neighbours, but mischief is in their hearts
p: We are given a constant stream of negativity, of liars and injustice, workers of iniquity, seemingly prospering. Even those that are caught and tried by the system are often given clemency and cover, to continue in their evil deeds.
p: We ask that God hear our voice, and to respond. To keep us separated from the wrath that is appointed to the workers of iniquity.
p: Those that speak peace but have mischief in their hearts are all around us. In the news and in our social media feeds. We have a constant black pilling from the world, and can get discouraged.

--- Render to them their desert
kicker: Psalm 28 · Give them according to their deeds
big: Psalm 28:4 | mark: render to them their desert
p: We also see the world waxing worse and worse, and the repercussions of past sins being visited on our generation, and it can overwhelm some with fear of being caught up in the collapse that is always just around the corner.
p: But our perspective should be to entreat the Lord with supplication, that as He punishes the wicked, He protects His people. We should want them to receive recompense for their evil deeds, and trust He will protect us from His wrath.

--- He shall destroy them
kicker: Psalm 28 · The works of the Lord
big: Psalm 28:5

--- My strength and my shield
kicker: Psalm 28 · The Lord hath heard
scripture: Psalm 28:6-8 | mark: The LORD is my strength and my shield
p: The Lord has heard us, and is our strength and shield, the rock of salvation. We should feel joy rather than fear, and praise Him openly for the destruction that comes upon the wicked.
p: Thy Kingdom come, O Lord. Even so, come quickly. Spare your anointed from your wrath. Save us out of our tribulation. Destroy the wicked.

--- Save thy people
kicker: Psalm 28 · Thine inheritance
big: Psalm 28:9
center: Amen

=== Psalm 29
--- Psalm 29 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 29
sub: The voice of the LORD
refs: Psalm 29:1

--- The voice of the Lord
kicker: Psalm 29 · His Word
scripture: Psalm 29:1-4 | mark: The voice of the LORD is powerful
p: Pure worship of the Lord, His voice, His Word. Christ Jesus is the name, and through Him God spoke all creation into existence, spoke via the prophets all scripture, the inspiration by God of all things. His word settled in Heaven, forever.

--- The Lord sitteth King for ever
kicker: Psalm 29 · The King
scripture: Psalm 29:5-10 | mark: the LORD sitteth King for ever
p: All glory is His, as all things are by Him. He sits as King, forever.

--- Peace
kicker: Psalm 29 · Strength unto his people
big: Psalm 29:11
p: *Give unto the LORD the glory due unto his name; worship the LORD in the beauty of holiness*, because His Word alone is the cause of all creation, and everything therein. And knowing His power and majesty, rejoice that *The LORD will give strength unto his people; the LORD will bless his people with peace.*
center: Amen!

=== Psalm 30
--- Psalm 30 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 30
sub: Joy cometh in the morning
refs: Psalm 30:1

--- Thou hast lifted me up
kicker: Psalm 30 · I will extol thee
scripture: Psalm 30:1-3 | mark: thou hast lifted me up; thou hast brought up my soul from the grave
p: O LORD! In Psalm 30, David extols the Lord for His gift of salvation, and calls all in the congregation to do the same.
p: God has lifted us up, heard us when we called upon His name, and healed us from our sin. We are brought up from the grave and shall never perish. Our souls will not see Hell. We shall not go down to the pit.

--- At the remembrance of his holiness
kicker: Psalm 30 · Sing unto the Lord
big: Psalm 30:4 | mark: give thanks at the remembrance of his holiness
p: Rejoice! This verse makes me think of the Lord's supper. The thanksgiving and remembrance of Jesus' holiness.
big: 1 Corinthians 11:24 | mark: this do in remembrance of me
p: This communal act of thanksgiving and memorial - *For as often as ye eat this bread, and drink this cup, ye do shew the Lord's death till he come*. For this we should sing unto the Lord, extolling His mercy, forever.

--- In his favour is life
kicker: Psalm 30 · His anger endureth but a moment
big: Psalm 30:5 | mark: in his favour is life; joy cometh in the morning
p: *For his anger endureth but a moment; in his favour is life*. The chastening of sons by the Lord is fleeting. But grace, the unmerited favor of God, is given to the world through His Son. And in His favor is life.
big: Romans 6:23 | mark: the gift of God is eternal life
p: What a wonderful promise. How can we not sing for joy and give Him glory?

--- I shall never be moved
kicker: Psalm 30 · By thy favour
scripture: Psalm 30:6-7 | mark: I shall never be moved
p: The comfort we have in Christ, knowing that *weeping may endure for a night, but joy cometh in the morning*, knowing for certain that in our prosperity, in the gift of God, we shall not be moved. Our enemies shall not overcome us. We are made to stand on a sure foundation, the rock that is Christ. By His favor. By His grace.
p: We can trust that no matter what we face, even if we have angered God, that by turning to Him and crying unto Him, we have access to His grace through faith and the mediator of that faith, the man Christ Jesus.

--- Shall the dust praise thee?
kicker: Psalm 30 · I cried to thee
scripture: Psalm 30:8-9 | mark: Shall the dust praise thee?
p: The psalmist calls on God, reminding Him of His grace and mercy, the favor with which He has blessed us. And one of the purposes of our salvation, our healing, our getting saved, is that we will live forever to sing His praise.
p: The resurrection is sure.
p: Some will say the context of the Psalm is thanksgiving for not being killed by David's enemies, and triumphantly giving credit to God, but ultimately it is a song extolling the grace of God that those who trust in Him shall never perish.

--- Lord, be thou my helper
kicker: Psalm 30 · Have mercy upon me
big: Psalm 30:10
p: *Hear, O LORD, and have mercy upon me: LORD, be thou my helper.* This is our prayer of salvation. Lord have mercy on me, a sinner. Knowing that God, Who cannot lie, has promised that all who call upon His name shall not perish, but be saved.

--- Mourning into dancing
kicker: Psalm 30 · To the end
scripture: Psalm 30:11-12 | mark: Thou hast turned for me my mourning into dancing; To the end that my glory may sing praise to thee
p: *Thou hast turned for me my mourning into dancing: thou hast put off my sackcloth, and girded me with gladness*...Jesus saves. Death has no sting for those in Christ. And while we may celebrate victory in this life, it is the victory of the cross that we should give thanks and praise, forever, because the purpose of God's sparing us, temporally or spiritually, is that we may sing His praise:
p: *To the end*, that is, the purpose of His salvation, our singing praise to God. Ultimately, that is what it is all about. Recognizing the glory and grace of a perfect and holy God. Amen

